talk(387,'1976-03-22','I. M. Gelfand','P000647-P000649','A probability distribution arising in biology',r'''A short intervention introduces what is now called the generalized Poisson distribution and sets its normalization as an exercise. The date and text are on P000648. The formula already appeared in print before this meeting; the notebook does not identify the biological model or the unnamed second example.''')
item(387,'P000648','definition','The generalized Poisson distribution',r'''For (0\leq\alpha<1), (P_n=e^{-x_0-\alpha n}x_0(x_0+\alpha n)^{n-1}/n!); at \(\alpha=0\) the distribution is Poisson.''',r'''Let \(x_0>0\), \(0\leq\alpha<1\), and \(n\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\). Set \(P_0=e^{-x_0}\) and use the displayed formula for \(n\ge1\). These numbers are nonnegative. The parameter range avoids the finite-support difficulties of negative deformation parameters.''',('consuljain1973poisson',),fate=r'''This matches \citet[Section 3, equation (3.1)]{consuljain1973poisson}, received in May 1971, revised in October 1972 and published in November 1973. The biological provenance in this intervention remains unspecified.''',publication_match='exact',area='Probability')
item(387,'P000648','question','Normalization of the generalized Poisson law',r'''The explicit exercise is to verify \(\sum_{n\ge0}P_n=1\).''',r'''Let \(T(z)\) be the nonnegative solution near zero of \(T=z\exp(\alpha(T-1))\). Lagrange inversion gives
\[\sum_{n\ge0}P_nz^n=\exp(x_0(T(z)-1)).\]
For \(0\leq\alpha<1\), continuity from below and the minimal fixed point at \(z=1\) give \(T(1)=1\), hence total mass one.''',('consuljain1973poisson',),reconstruction=r'''The generating-function proof is supplied here; it is not written in the notes. For \(\alpha=0\), \(T=z\). For \(0<\alpha<1\), strict convexity of \(e^{\alpha(t-1)}-t\) excludes a fixed point below one.''',fate=r'''Normalization belongs to the already published distribution \citep[Sections 2--3]{consuljain1973poisson}. Thus this is an expository exercise, not a problem first solved after the seminar.''',problem='GP-1975-22',publication_match='exact',area='Probability')
problem('GP-1975-22','Normalization of the generalized Poisson law',r'''For \(x_0>0\) and \(0\le\alpha<1\), prove that \(P_0=e^{-x_0}\), \(P_n=e^{-x_0-\alpha n}x_0(x_0+\alpha n)^{n-1}/n!\) have total mass one.''',['1975-387.2'],r'''Solved before the meeting in the generalized Poisson literature (1973); the occurrence includes an elementary generating-function reconstruction. Status checked November 2026.''',['consuljain1973poisson'])
save()

talk(388,'1976-03-22','A. V. Roiter','P000647-P000650','Indecomposable modules and root systems',r'''Roiter discusses the first Brauer--Thrall theorem, chains of indecomposable submodules and a comparison of lengths. The discussion then turns to integral quadratic forms, finite and affine Dynkin diagrams, reflections and arithmetic root criteria. Kirillov's drawing of Dynkin diagrams is an intervention within this record. A final star-shaped form raises the problem of enumerating roots by reflections.''')
item(388,'P000648','theorem','The first Brauer–Thrall theorem',r'''A finite-dimensional algebra has either finitely many indecomposable modules or indecomposables of arbitrarily large dimension. Roiter is named, followed by an Auslander generalization to Artinian rings.''',r'''For a finite-dimensional algebra \(\Lambda\) over a field, bounded dimensions of finite-dimensional indecomposable modules imply that there are only finitely many isomorphism classes of such modules. The corresponding formulation for Artin algebras uses composition length. The phrase ``Artinian rings'' needs the precise finiteness setting of the theorem being invoked.''',('roiter1968brauerthrall','ringel2006foundation'),fate=r'''Roiter's article was submitted on 20 March 1968 and published that year \citep{roiter1968brauerthrall}. A later proof within Artin-algebra representation theory is given by \citet[Section 6]{ringel2006foundation}; no claim about arbitrary left Artinian rings is inferred from that proof.''',publication_match='exact',area='Representation theory')
item(388,'P000648','claim','Infinite chains of indecomposable modules',r'''The notebook asks for or asserts an infinite chain \(A_1\subset A_2\subset\cdots\) with indecomposable terms in the infinite case.''',r'''If an Artin algebra is of infinite representation type, there is an infinite sequence of finite-length indecomposables and proper monomorphisms \(M_1\hookrightarrow M_2\hookrightarrow\cdots\). The chain can be chosen to consist of Gabriel--Roiter inclusions; its lengths are unbounded.''',('ringel2006foundation',),**{'class':'B'},fate=r'''A precise strengthened form is \citet[Section 7.3, Theorem]{ringel2006foundation}, published in 2006 after lectures in 2003--2005. The source does not specify the inclusions, and its exact contemporary publication was not identified.''',publication_match='partial',area='Representation theory')
item(388,'P000648','theorem','Bounding composition length by indecomposable-chain length',r'''Besides composition length \(\ell(M)\), the notes define \(\bar\ell(M)\) as the largest number of terms in a chain of indecomposable submodules. Their quantifier order is \(\forall N\,\exists n:\bar\ell(M)\le n\Rightarrow\ell(M)\le N\).''',r'''The substantive uniform bound is: for each Artin algebra \(\Lambda\) and each \(n\), there exists \(N(\Lambda,n)\) such that \(\bar\ell(M)\le n\) implies \(\ell(M)\le N(\Lambda,n)\), for indecomposable finite-length \(M\). If \(p,q\) are the maximum lengths of indecomposable projectives and injectives, one may take \(N=(pq)^{n-1}\): a Gabriel--Roiter filtration starts with a simple module, and each inclusion multiplies length by at most \(pq\).''',('ringel2006foundation',),**{'class':'B'},reconstruction=r'''The implication printed in the notes is not this bound; it is too weak to express the intended control. The exponent is a direct deduction from the cited filtration and inclusion bound, not a formula attributed to the notebook.''',fate=r'''Use \citet[Section 1.5 and Section 3.1, Corollary]{ringel2006foundation}. These establish the ingredients in the Artin-algebra setting; this is a later rigorous match to the intended estimate, not an exact identification of Roiter's wording.''',literal_correctness='not determinable',area='Representation theory')
item(388,'P000648-P000649','definition','Integral forms and finite or affine Dynkin diagrams',r'''The form \(q(x)=\sum a_ix_i^2+\sum_{i<j}b_{ij}x_ix_j\) has \(a_i>0\), \(b_{ij}\le0\), and \(a_i\mid b_{ij}\). Positivity is associated with Dynkin diagrams; nonnegativity with extended diagrams. Kirillov draws the finite families.''',r'''Assume integer coefficients, divisibility at both endpoints, and connected coefficient graph. The matrix \(C_{ii}=2\), \(C_{ij}=b_{ij}/a_i\) is a symmetrizable generalized Cartan matrix. Positive definiteness gives finite type; positive semidefiniteness with a nontrivial radical gives affine type. Nonnegativity alone includes finite type, and disconnected forms require a componentwise statement.''',('bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter',),reconstruction=r'''Connectedness and singularity distinguish the affine assertion. The cited source directly treats the simply laced representation-theoretic part; the Cartan-matrix translation of the displayed form is an explicit reconstruction.''',fate=r'''The simply laced correspondence and its use in quiver classification predate the meeting: \citet[Section 3]{bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter}. This connects thematically to GP-1972-301 and GP-1972-302, but no repeated quiver-classification question is written here.''',speaker='A. V. Roiter; A. A. Kirillov (intervention)',area='Lie theory')
item(388,'P000649','construction','Reflections and roots of simply laced forms',r'''A reflection changes one coordinate and preserves \(q\); roots are obtained from coordinate vectors. In the equal-coefficient case, \(q(x)=1\) is the root criterion.''',r'''For \(a_i=1\), define \(s_i(x)=x-(2x_i+\sum_{j\ne i}b_{ij}x_j)e_i\). For a finite simply laced Dynkin form, the integral solutions of \(q(x)=1\) are precisely the real roots \(\{w(e_i):w\in\langle s_1,\ldots,s_n\rangle\}\). In affine type the same norm condition characterizes real roots; nonzero imaginary roots have \(q=0\) and must be listed separately.''',('bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter',),fate=r'''The finite-type reflection argument is already in \citet[Section 3]{bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter}. The word ``roots'' in the notes is orbit-defined, and should not silently be enlarged to all imaginary roots.''',publication_match='partial',area='Lie theory')
item(388,'P000649','claim','A divisibility criterion for roots',r'''For the more general form, the notebook states that \(x\) is a root if and only if \(q(x)\) divides every \(a_ix_i\).''',r'''The proposed arithmetic criterion is \(q(x)\mid a_ix_i\) for all \(i\), for a nonzero integral vector of a finite crystallographic form. Its literal extension to singular forms is undefined when \(q(x)=0\), and the source gives no convention or proof. The statement is retained as a claim; an exact published theorem with the indicated hypotheses was not identified.''',(),**{'class':'F'},fate='Searches for Roiter, quadratic forms, divisibility and root criteria located the standard reflection theory but no exact source for this formulation. No unsupported attribution to a named theorem is made.',present_status='As of November 2026, the intended finite-type arithmetic statement remains an identification gap in this companion, not an asserted open research problem.',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Lie theory')
item(388,'P000649','question','Roots of the star-shaped quadratic form',r'''The final example is \(q=x_0^2+\cdots+x_n^2-x_0(x_1+\cdots+x_n)\); the question is to obtain all roots by elementary reflections, illustrated by a word-changing analogy.''',r'''The orbit of the coordinate vectors under the reflections attached to this star is a precise enumerable set of real roots. Completing squares gives \(q=\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-x_0/2)^2+(1-n/4)x_0^2\): the cases \(n\le3\) are finite, \(n=4\) is affine \(\widetilde D_4\), and \(n\ge5\) is indefinite. An exhaustive finite list is therefore not an appropriate interpretation for all \(n\).''',('bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter',),reconstruction=r'''The square completion and the distinction between finite enumeration and an orbit algorithm are supplied here. The notes do not state a bound on word length or demand an algorithm deciding all imaginary roots.''',fate=r'''The reflection machinery is already available in \citet[Sections 2--3]{bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter}. This question is understood as an exercise in that machinery; no separate unresolved classification conjecture is inferred.''',publication_match='partial',area='Lie theory')
correction('P000648','quantifiers in the length theorem','for every N there exists n','for every n there exists N(Λ,n), in the intended uniform bound','The scan confirms the printed quantifiers. The replacement is a mathematical reconstruction supported by the Gabriel–Roiter filtration bound, not a transcription repair.')
save()

talk(389,'1976-03-22','A. A. Kirillov','P000648-P000650','Berezin’s analysis with anticommuting variables',r'''Kirillov presents superfunctions, integration and transformations, then graded Lie algebras and a Lie correspondence. Gelfand adds a nilpotent Taylor formula. A final question asks for deformations in the direction of a Poisson bracket. The unnamed 64-dimensional algebra and its particle interpretation cannot be identified from the supplied text.''')
item(389,'P000649','definition','Superfunctions and split supermanifolds',r'''Functions depend smoothly on commuting variables \(x\) and polynomially on anticommuting variables \(\xi\). A vector-bundle description is attributed to Rudakov.''',r'''A smooth real supermanifold of dimension \(p|q\) is a locally ringed space locally isomorphic to \((U,C^\infty_U\otimes\Lambda^\bullet\mathbb R^q)\). A split example is \((M,\Gamma(\Lambda E^*))\) for a rank-\(q\) vector bundle \(E\to M\). The local model does not itself select a canonical global splitting.''',('berezinleites1975supermanifolds',),fate=r'''The sheaf definition was published by \citet[pp. 505--506]{berezinleites1975supermanifolds} in 1975, following submission on 24 March 1975. The attribution to Rudakov is preserved as the seminar's attribution, without inferring a separate publication.''',publication_match='partial',area='Supergeometry')
item(389,'P000649','definition','Berezin integration',r'''Integration is specified by \(\int d\xi=0\) and \(\int\xi,d\xi=1\), extending to products of anticommuting variables.''',r'''For an ordered set of odd generators, Berezin integration is coefficient extraction: \(\int d\xi_q\cdots d\xi_1\,f\) is the coefficient of \(\xi_1\cdots\xi_q\). Changing the order changes signs. On a supermanifold, coordinate-invariant integration requires the Berezinian transformation law, rather than an ordinary volume form.''',('berezinleites1975supermanifolds','berezinkac1970lie'),fate=r'''The integration framework predates this meeting; see \citet[Section 5]{berezinkac1970lie} and \citet[pp. 507--508]{berezinleites1975supermanifolds}.''',area='Supergeometry')
item(389,'P000649','construction','Coordinate changes with nilpotent terms',r'''The examples include changes of an even coordinate by a product of odd coordinates, such as \(x\mapsto x+\xi_1\xi_2\), as well as transformations of the odd variables.''',r'''A morphism is determined locally by parity-preserving substitutions: even coordinates are sent to even superfunctions and odd coordinates to odd ones. A local isomorphism requires invertibility of the ordinary even Jacobian and of the linear odd part after nilpotents are discarded.''',('berezinleites1975supermanifolds',),fate=r'''These are the local morphisms in the sheaf-theoretic construction of \citet[pp. 505--506]{berezinleites1975supermanifolds}. Nilpotent corrections explain why a bundle picture does not exhaust the coordinate transformations.''',area='Supergeometry')
item(389,'P000649','remark','An unnamed 64-dimensional algebra',r'''The notes mention a 64-dimensional algebra that is said to describe particles better than \(SU(3)\), without naming its generators or representation.''',r'''Dimension alone does not determine a Lie superalgebra, its parity dimensions, real form, representation or physical model. No definite mathematical identification follows.''',(),**{'class':'F'},fate='Targeted searches on Berezin, Kirillov, 64-dimensional superalgebras and particle models did not identify a source matching all of these details.',present_status='As of November 2026, this remains an unresolved identification in the historical record.',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Supergeometry')
item(389,'P000649','definition','Lie superalgebras and associative envelopes',r'''The bracket is graded skew-symmetric, satisfies graded Jacobi, and is obtained from multiplication by a signed commutator; a converse is mentioned.''',r'''Over a field of characteristic zero, a Lie superalgebra \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g_0\oplus\mathfrak g_1\) satisfies \([x,y]=-(-1)^{|x||y|}[y,x]\) and the graded Jacobi identity. Every associative superalgebra has this bracket. Conversely \(\mathfrak g\) embeds in its universal enveloping superalgebra, whose associated graded algebra is \(S(\mathfrak g_0)\otimes\Lambda(\mathfrak g_1)\).''',('berezinkac1970lie',),reconstruction=r'''The precise converse is an enveloping-algebra statement, not an assertion that every Lie superalgebra already has an associative multiplication on its own vector space.''',fate=r'''The definitions and representation framework are present in \citet[Sections 3--4]{berezinkac1970lie}, published in 1970. The enveloping formulation spells out the otherwise ambiguous converse.''',publication_match='partial',area='Lie theory')
item(389,'P000649','theorem','The formal Lie correspondence in supergeometry',r'''A ``Lie theorem'' is attributed to Berezin and Kac ``from Kyiv''. No triangularization statement is written.''',r'''Over characteristic zero, finite-dimensional Lie superalgebras correspond to formal supergroup laws, with homomorphisms respected. This is a local/formal Lie correspondence; it does not assert that every representation of a solvable Lie superalgebra can be triangularized.''',('berezinkac1970lie',),reconstruction=r'''The identity of Kac is G. I. Kac, coauthor of the 1970 article, rather than an automatic identification with V. G. Kac. The theorem is interpreted using that precise paper.''',fate=r'''\citet[Section 3.5, Theorem 4]{berezinkac1970lie} proves the mutually inverse functors. This is an exact and pre-existing match to the attributed Lie correspondence.''',publication_match='exact',area='Lie theory')
item(389,'P000649','remark','Gelfand’s nilpotent Taylor formula',r'''Gelfand writes \(p(t+\xi)=p(t)+p'(t)\xi\).''',r'''For a polynomial \(p\) over a characteristic-zero commutative coefficient ring and a commuting nilpotent element \(\xi\) with \(\xi^2=0\), the binomial formula gives \(p(t+\xi)=p(t)+p'(t)\xi\). The same finite Taylor substitution is meaningful for smooth superfunctions with nilpotent increments. If \(t\) is even and \(\xi\) odd, this substitution by itself is not a parity-preserving even-coordinate change.''',(),fate='The identity is elementary; the parity qualification separates the algebraic Taylor example from the preceding definition of supermanifold morphism.',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',publication_match='none found',area='Supergeometry')
item(389,'P000650','question','Deformation in the direction of a super-Poisson bracket',r'''The notes end by asking to find deformations of the function superalgebra in the direction of its Poisson bracket.''',r'''A precise even-bracket interpretation asks for an associative parity-preserving product \(f*g=fg+\sum_{r\ge1}\hbar^rB_r(f,g)\) such that the first graded commutator is \(\hbar\{f,g\}+O(\hbar^2)\). For a split smooth supermanifold built from a symplectic base and a vector bundle with a nondegenerate symmetric fibre metric, the associated Rothstein even super-Poisson bracket has such a differential star product.''',('bordemann2000superpoisson',),**{'class':'B'},reconstruction=r'''Parity of the bracket, formal versus convergent deformation and nondegeneracy are absent from the notebook. The stated theorem is a definite later answer to an explicitly restricted interpretation, not a claim that all possible readings were equivalent.''',fate=r'''\citet[Theorems 1.3--1.4 and Corollary 1.1]{bordemann2000superpoisson}, first circulated on 30 March 2000, constructs the star product and identifies its first bracket. Odd brackets require a different formulation and are not covered by this identification.''',present_status='As of November 2026, the stated even symplectic interpretation is solved; the notes do not specify a unique more general problem.',problem='GP-1975-23',publication_match='partial',area='Deformation quantization')
problem('GP-1975-23','Deforming a superfunction algebra along an even Poisson bracket',r'''Construct a formal associative superproduct whose first graded commutator is a given even super-Poisson bracket. The notebook leaves the geometric and parity hypotheses unspecified; the definite version treated here is the Rothstein bracket on a split supermanifold over a symplectic base.''',['1975-389.8'],r'''The specified symplectic version has a Fedosov-type construction, circulated in 2000. This is a partial identification of the notebook question, not a claim about arbitrary odd or degenerate brackets. Status November 2026.''',['bordemann2000superpoisson'])
save()

talk(390,'1976-03-22','Ya. G. Sinai','P000648-P000651','Transfer matrices, the Ising model and square ice',r'''Sinai explains how a two-dimensional lattice partition function becomes a trace of a transfer matrix. He connects the limiting free energy to the largest eigenvalue, recalls Onsager's solution and its anticommuting-operator structure, and mentions Lieb's square-ice entropy calculation. The surviving mathematical text is on P000650.''')
item(390,'P000650','construction','The row transfer matrix for the Ising model',r'''Rows are vectors \(x,y\in\{\pm1\}^n\), and \(A(x,y)=\exp\{\beta[J_1\sum x_ix_{i-1}+J_2\sum x_iy_i]\}\), with periodic indices.''',r'''For an \(n\times R\) torus with nearest-neighbour horizontal and vertical couplings and no external field, the partition function is \(Z_{n,R}=\operatorname{tr}(A_n^R)\). Assigning all horizontal interactions to the first row produces the nonsymmetric matrix in the notes; splitting them equally gives a similar symmetric matrix. Each bond must be counted once.''',('onsager1944crystal',),fate=r'''The finite-width transfer-operator problem is solved in \citet[Sections II--V]{onsager1944crystal}, received 4 October 1943 and published in 1944. The identity \(Z=\operatorname{tr}A^R\) follows immediately by summing cyclic row sequences.''',publication_match='exact',area='Statistical mechanics')
item(390,'P000650','method','Free energy and the thermodynamic limit',r'''The probability is proportional to \(e^{-\beta H}\), and the notes identify free energy through \(\lim R^{-2}\log Z\), with singularities associated with the dominant eigenvalue.''',r'''For a finite system the Gibbs probability is \(e^{-\beta H}/Z\). At fixed width \(n\), positivity gives \(\lim_{R\to\infty}R^{-1}\log Z_{n,R}=\log\lambda_{\max}(A_n)\). A two-dimensional pressure requires a further width limit, or a joint limit with both side lengths tending to infinity. Finite positive transfer matrices have a simple dominant eigenvalue analytic in finite real couplings; a phase singularity arises only in an infinite-size limit.''',('onsager1944crystal',),reconstruction=r'''The source uses the word free energy for the dimensionless pressure; physical free energy per site is its negative divided by \(\beta\). The distinction between fixed width and a two-dimensional limit is essential.''',fate=r'''\citet[abstract and Section IX]{onsager1944crystal} explicitly separates finite strips from the infinite crystal. The normalization here is fixed by the elementary Gibbs definition.''',area='Statistical mechanics')
item(390,'P000650','theorem','Onsager’s zero-field Ising solution',r'''The eigenvalues of the transfer matrix are attributed to Onsager, with a singularity at a distinguished temperature.''',r'''For the infinite anisotropic square-lattice Ising ferromagnet with \(J_1,J_2>0\) and zero magnetic field, the critical inverse temperature is characterized by \(\sinh(2\beta_cJ_1)\sinh(2\beta_cJ_2)=1\). The exact free energy is nonanalytic there; the internal energy is continuous and the specific heat has a logarithmic divergence.''',('onsager1944crystal',),fate=r'''These conclusions are in the original \citet[abstract and Section IX]{onsager1944crystal}. No statement about a nonzero magnetic field is warranted by the notes.''',publication_match='exact',area='Statistical mechanics')
item(390,'P000650','method','Anticommuting operators in the transfer-matrix solution',r'''Operators \(A_i\) anticommute and the transfer matrix is written schematically as \(\exp(\sum c_{ij}A_iA_j)\).''',r'''A Clifford algebra organizes the zero-field transfer calculation. For periodic spin boundary conditions, fermion-parity sectors must be treated separately, and a scalar normalization may multiply the exponential of a quadratic Clifford expression. One should not read the schematic equality as a global sector-free normal form for every transfer matrix.''',('onsager1944crystal',),fate=r'''The underlying algebra appears in \citet[Sections IV--VII]{onsager1944crystal}. The notebook compresses normalization and boundary-sector information, so the publication match is structural rather than a literal equality.''',publication_match='partial',area='Statistical mechanics')
item(390,'P000650','claim','Lieb’s entropy of square ice',r'''Lieb is credited with finding the entropy for a model of ice.''',r'''For the equal-weight square-ice model, each vertex has two incoming and two outgoing arrows. In the thermodynamic limit its number of states per vertex is \(W=(4/3)^{3/2}\), giving entropy per vertex \(k_B\log W\). This identifies the two-dimensional six-vertex model; it is not an exact entropy formula for three-dimensional physical ice.''',('lieb1967squareice',),fate=r'''The detailed result is \citet[abstract and final entropy formula]{lieb1967squareice}, published on 5 October 1967 after a shorter April announcement. The original full text is a source target; publisher metadata and the result stated in the abstract are verified.''',publication_match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Statistical mechanics')
save()

talk(391,'1976-03-29','I. M. Krichever','P000649-P000654','Higher-rank commuting operators; Shnirelman on spectral phases',r'''The catalogue names Krichever, but the manuscript changes speakers on P000651: Krichever and Drinfeld discuss the inverse construction of commuting ordinary differential operators, then Shnirelman discusses spectral phases, closed geodesics and the wave trace through P000653. Arnold's question about nodal sets occurs within the latter discussion. These changes are retained as speaker fields, not promoted to invented catalogue records. The first part continues the preceding algebro-geometric line; the second revisits 1973-333.18--20.''')
item(391,'P000651','construction','The spectral curve and joint-eigenfunction sheaf',r'''For commuting scalar ordinary differential operators \(L_1,L_2\), the joint eigenvalues satisfy \(P(E_1,E_2)=0\). Coprime orders give a line bundle; orders four and six suggest a rank-two bundle.''',r'''A commutative ring \(\mathcal A\) of ordinary scalar differential operators of positive order has an algebraic spectral curve, with a distinguished point at infinity. In the generic smooth case, common eigenspaces define a vector bundle of rank \(r\), the greatest common divisor of the orders of all nonzero operators in \(\mathcal A\). Coprime orders force \(r=1\); the gcd of a selected pair need not equal the rank of a larger ring.''',('krichever1978commutative',),reconstruction=r'''The hypotheses are local smooth/analytic coefficients with nonvanishing leading coefficients, followed by a suitable normalization; singular spectral curves require torsion-free sheaves. The source's two selected orders do not by themselves fix the rank of every containing ring.''',fate=r'''\citet[Theorem 1.1 and Section 1]{krichever1978commutative} recalls the pre-existing spectral-curve construction and clarifies the spectral data. The 1978 paper distinguishes this direct construction from the inverse problem.''',publication_match='exact',area='Integrable systems')
item(391,'P000651','claim','Counting eigenvectors over a fixed spectral value',r'''After saying that the coprime case is one-dimensional, the notes say that the number of common eigenfunctions is \(n\), with distinct eigenvalues.''',r'''If \(\operatorname{ord}L_1=n\), then \(\ker(L_1-E_1)\) is locally \(n\)-dimensional. The commuting \(L_2\) acts on this space. For generic \(E_1\) and rank one, it has \(n\) distinct eigenvalues, each with a one-dimensional joint eigenspace. Thus there are \(n\) points above a generic \(E_1\), not an \(n\)-dimensional eigenspace at one point \((E_1,E_2)\).''',('krichever1978commutative',),reconstruction=r'''This separates the degree of the spectral projection from the rank of the eigenfunction bundle, resolving the apparent contradiction on the page.''',fate=r'''The solution-space construction used in the proof of \citet[Theorem 1.1]{krichever1978commutative} supplies precisely this distinction.''',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Integrable systems')
item(391,'P000651','question','The higher-rank inverse construction',r'''The inverse problem is explicitly marked ``not done'', with orders four and six as the rank-two example: construct commuting differential operators from the bundle.''',r'''Given an appropriate smooth pointed spectral curve, rank-\(r\) bundle data, a local parameter and a compatible framing/asymptotic prescription at the marked point, reconstruct a commutative ring of scalar ordinary differential operators. A bare bundle without this extra data is not sufficient. In generic position, rank \(r\) also involves \(r-1\) functions of the independent variable after normalization.''',('krichever1978commutative',),**{'class':'A'},fate=r'''\citet[Theorem 2.3]{krichever1978commutative}, submitted 15 February 1977 and published in 1978, completes the generic smooth-curve construction. Its introduction expressly corrects errors in the 1977 implementation. This is a subsequent answer under stated generic hypotheses, not an unrestricted classification of all singular cases.''',present_status='As of November 2026, the generic inverse correspondence described here is established; the notebook’s unrestricted shorthand must retain the spectral-data qualifications.',problem='GP-1975-26',publication_match='exact',area='Integrable systems')
item(391,'P000651','claim','Drinfeld’s functional parameters and gauge normalization',r'''Drinfeld says \(d=(m,n)\) leaves \(d-1\) arbitrary functions; conjugation removes one coefficient, while a leading coefficient remains. Powers \(Q^2,Q^3\) already give order-four/order-six examples with two functions.''',r'''After local change of independent variable and scalar conjugation, a monic order-\(r\) auxiliary operator can be normalized to \(\partial^r+v_{r-2}(x)\partial^{r-2}+\cdots+v_0(x)\), leaving \(r-1\) functions. Spectral curve and bundle parameters remain in addition. For an arbitrary order-two \(Q=a_2\partial^2+a_1\partial+a_0\), scalar conjugation can remove the first-order term locally; if coordinate changes are not also factored out, \(a_2\) remains alongside the potential.''',('krichever1978commutative',),**{'class':'A'},reconstruction=r'''The phrase ``specifying a pair iff specifying functions'' suppresses finite-dimensional spectral data and the equivalence relation. The example \(Q^2,Q^3\) explains why the choice of normalization matters.''',fate=r'''The normalized \(r-1\)-function statement is made precise by \citet[Theorem 2.3]{krichever1978commutative}. It applies to the ring rank, not automatically to the gcd of any chosen pair in a larger ring.''',speaker='V. G. Drinfeld',publication_match='partial',area='Integrable systems')
problem('GP-1975-26','Reconstructing higher-rank commuting ordinary differential operators',r'''Recover a commutative ring of scalar ordinary differential operators from generic pointed spectral-curve and rank-\(r\) bundle data, with the framing and functional parameters required at infinity; orders four and six are the recorded motivating example.''',['1975-391.3','1975-391.4'],r'''The generic smooth-curve inverse construction was completed in Krichever’s article submitted in February 1977 and published in 1978, with corrections to the preceding implementation. Status November 2026.''',['krichever1978commutative'])
item(391,'P000651','definition','Laplacian convention and Weyl frequencies',r'''Shnirelman takes a compact analytic Riemannian manifold, writes \(\Delta u_k+\lambda_k^2u_k=0\), defines \(\Delta\) through spherical averages, and invokes \(\lambda_k\sim c k^{1/n}\).''',r'''Let \(M\) be a closed smooth Riemannian \(n\)-manifold and use the nonpositive Laplace--Beltrami operator. Then \(-\Delta u_k=\lambda_k^2u_k\), with \(\lambda_k\ge0\), counted with multiplicity. Weyl's law is
\[N(\Lambda)\sim\frac{\omega_n\operatorname{Vol}(M)}{(2\pi)^n}\Lambda^n.\]
For normalized averages over small geodesic spheres, \(\Delta f=2n\lim_{\tau\to0}(\operatorname{avg}_{S_\tau}f-f)/\tau^2\).''',('duistermaatguillemin1975spectrum',),reconstruction=r'''Closedness supplies the missing boundary hypothesis. The factor \(2n\) is obtained by a normal-coordinate Taylor expansion. Analyticity is stronger than needed for the Weyl law.''',fate=r'''The relevant positive elliptic spectral setting already appears in \citet[opening hypotheses]{duistermaatguillemin1975spectrum}; the sphere normalization is reconstructed directly. Publication metadata, rather than the original full proof, was read.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000651','example' if False else 'construction','The rectangular flat-torus spectrum',r'''The example uses a flat torus and frequencies \(\lambda^2=n^2/a^2+m^2/b^2\); one displayed eigenvalue equation reverses the earlier sign of \(\Delta\).''',r'''On \(\mathbb R^2/(2\pi a\mathbb Z\times2\pi b\mathbb Z)\), the functions \(e^{i(nx/a+my/b)}\) have eigenvalues \(n^2/a^2+m^2/b^2\) for \(-\Delta\). Multiplicities and the zero mode must be retained when listing the frequencies. The positive-sign equation is for \(-\Delta\), not the previously defined \(\Delta\).''',(),fate='This is an elementary separation-of-variables calculation. It supplies a test case for the spectral-phase question, but its highly degenerate closed geodesics do not satisfy the later isolated-orbit hypotheses.',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',publication_match='none found',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000651-P000652','question','Abel-renormalized distributions of spectral phases',r'''The question concerns \(a\lambda_k\pmod{2\pi}\). The proposed distribution subtracts its constant Fourier mode and multiplies an exponentially smoothed spectral sum by \(\varepsilon\).''',r'''With \(\delta_{\rm per}\) the periodic delta distribution and \(a>0\), define
\[\Phi_{a,\varepsilon}(\mu)=\varepsilon\sum_k e^{-\varepsilon\lambda_k}\left(\delta_{\rm per}(\mu-a\lambda_k)-\frac1{2\pi}\right).\]
Ask whether \(\Phi_{a,\varepsilon}\) has a limit in \(\mathcal D'(\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z)\), and how its Fourier coefficients depend on closed geodesics. This is a signed renormalized distribution, not the probability distribution of the first \(N\) phases.''',('duistermaatguillemin1975spectrum','zelditch1998wave'),**{'class':'C'},reconstruction=r'''The precise topology and the sign convention in the Fourier transform are supplied here. The \(m\ne0\) coefficient is \(\varepsilon(2\pi)^{-1}\operatorname{tr}e^{-(\varepsilon+ima)\sqrt{-\Delta}}\); its individual limit and distributional convergence are different questions.''',fate=r'''The closed-orbit interpretation is supported by the wave-trace expansion, already developed in 1975 and explicitly recalled by \citet[Introduction]{zelditch1998wave}. No located source proves the entire global distributional assertion in exactly the notebook's generality.''',present_status='As of November 2026, the fixed-Fourier-mode interpretation is identified; a theorem with sufficient uniform bounds for the notebook’s full Abel limit was not located in the targeted searches.',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',problem='GP-1975-24',worth='A bounded development should first distinguish convergence of each Fourier coefficient from uniform polynomial bounds needed for a distributional limit.',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000652','claim','Closed-geodesic resonances in spectral phases',r'''Under a genericity assumption, the notes say \(\Phi_a\ne0\) precisely when \(a\) is a rational multiple of a closed-geodesic length.''',r'''At a fixed nonzero Fourier index \(m\), a possible contribution occurs when \(|m|a\) is a closed-orbit length. For finitely many isolated nondegenerate orbits of that length, the leading coefficient is a sum of their principal wave invariants. Nonvanishing requires this sum not to cancel. ``Distinct incommensurable lengths'' can only refer to primitive unoriented geodesics: iterates and orientation reversal already force relations.''',('duistermaatguillemin1975spectrum','zelditch1998wave'),reconstruction=r'''This is a justified local interpretation of the resonance statement. It does not assert a global distributional ``if and only if'' without the convergence and noncancellation hypotheses of the preceding item.''',fate=r'''The singularities and their orbital coefficients belong to the pre-existing wave-trace theory; \citet[Introduction]{zelditch1998wave}. The exact Shnirelman publication for this Abel formulation remains unidentified.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000652','definition','The symplectic return map and its spectral types',r'''A closed geodesic has a Poincaré map; its multipliers are listed as reciprocal real pairs, unit-circle pairs, and complex quadruples, with the last type associated with dimension three.''',r'''The linearized return map acts symplectically on a transverse space of dimension \(2n-2\). Its nonreal, non-unit multipliers occur in quadruples \(\lambda,\bar\lambda,\lambda^{-1},\bar\lambda^{-1}\); real and unit-circle pairs give hyperbolic and elliptic blocks. A quadruple requires \(n\ge3\), not \(n=3\) exclusively. Nondegeneracy at an iterate means \(\det(I-P_\gamma^k)\ne0\).''',('zelditch1998wave',),fate=r'''The block decomposition and nondegenerate setting are used in \citet[Section 1]{zelditch1998wave}. The reciprocal-pair assertion also follows directly from symplectic linear algebra.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000652','claim','Elliptic and hyperbolic orbit amplitudes',r'''The coefficients are written with denominators \(\sin(k\alpha/2)\), \(\sinh(k\alpha/2)\), and \((-1)^k/\sinh(k\alpha/2)\) for three multiplier types.''',r'''The magnitude of a nondegenerate block's contribution is proportional to \(|\det(I-P^k)|^{-1/2}\), with a separate Maslov phase. For multipliers \(e^{\pm i\alpha}\) it is \((2|\sin(k\alpha/2)|)^{-1}\); for \(e^{\pm\alpha}\), \((2|\sinh(k\alpha/2)|)^{-1}\). For \(-e^{\pm\alpha}\), even \(k\) gives the hyperbolic sine denominator, while odd \(k\) gives \(2\cosh(k\alpha/2)\).''',('zelditch1998wave',),reconstruction=r'''These three magnitudes are obtained directly by multiplying \((1-\lambda^k)(1-\lambda^{-k})\). A factor \((-1)^k\) cannot replace the sine/cosine distinction in the negative-real case.''',fate=r'''The determinant and phase organization is the metaplectic form recalled in \citet[Introduction, equation (0.1)]{zelditch1998wave}. The block calculation corrects the literal manuscript formula.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',literal_correctness='false as written',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000652','construction','Regularizing the elliptic delta comb',r'''A regularized sum of deltas at half-integer multiples of an angle is written with the damping factor and constant subtraction inside the argument of \(\delta\). The preceding line explicitly requires the angle to be poorly approximable by rationals.''',r'''One coherent reconstruction is
\[D_{\alpha,\varepsilon}=\sum_{r\ge0}e^{-\varepsilon r}\left(\delta_{\rm per}(\mu-(r+\tfrac12)\alpha)-\frac1{2\pi}\right).\]
For \(m\ne0\), its Fourier coefficient is \((2\pi)^{-1}e^{-im\alpha/2}/(1-e^{-\varepsilon-im\alpha})\). If \(|1-e^{-im\alpha}|^{-1}\) grows at most polynomially, these coefficients converge to a distribution; a Diophantine condition on \(\alpha/2\pi\) suffices. Irrationality alone does not supply that bound.''',(),reconstruction=r'''The placement of damping and subtraction is emended to make a standard Abel sum. The Fourier coefficients follow from a geometric series and show exactly where a small-divisor hypothesis enters. The notebook does supply a poor-approximation hypothesis; the polynomial Fourier bound makes its required role precise.''',fate='The stated regularization and convergence criterion are derived here directly. No exact publication match to the manuscript’s misplaced parentheses was found.',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='none found',verification='read the result',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000653','remark','Hyperbolic continuation and an elliptic-function sketch',r'''The hyperbolic case is illustrated with poles and a reference to a Weierstrass function, but period and scale normalizations are not recorded.''',r'''A hyperbolic-block sequence has exponentially decaying coefficients such as \(1/\sinh(k\alpha/2)\). It therefore defines a periodic analytic function in a nonzero complex strip. A meromorphic expression in terms of elliptic functions would additionally require its periods, residues and additive normalization.''',(),**{'class':'F'},fate='The sketch does not identify a unique Weierstrass function or an exact identity. Searches on Shnirelman, spectral phases, hyperbolic multipliers and Weierstrass functions did not recover that identity.',present_status='As of November 2026, the exact formula remains an identification gap; exponential convergence of the reconstructed Fourier series is elementary.',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000653','claim','Holomorphic half-wave trace versus meromorphic continuation',r'''For \(f(z)=\sum e^{i\lambda_kz}\), the notes claim downward analytic continuation with simple poles at geodesic lengths and identify residues with Fourier coefficients.''',r'''For \(\operatorname{Im}z>0\), the sum converges normally on compact subsets by Weyl's bound. Its boundary value is the distributional half-wave trace. At an isolated nondegenerate nonzero closed-orbit length, its leading singularity has simple-pole type, but lower terms generally include powers times logarithms. At zero the leading singularity has order \(n\). These facts do not yield global meromorphic continuation through the real axis.''',('zelditch1998wave',),fate=r'''The singularity expansion in \citet[Introduction, first displayed formula]{zelditch1998wave} contains the logarithmic terms absent from the notebook. This continues the wave-trace discussion of occurrences 1973-333.18--20.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000651','theorem','Sturm’s bound for linear combinations',r'''The discussion recalls a one-dimensional bound on the zeros of a linear combination of the first eigenfunctions and compares it with polynomials.''',r'''For a regular real Sturm--Liouville problem on a compact interval with separated self-adjoint boundary conditions, order the simple eigenvalues increasingly. A nonzero linear combination of eigenfunctions with indices \(m,\ldots,n\) has at least \(m-1\) sign changes and at most \(n-1\) interior zeros, with multiplicities treated in the precise Sturm version. In particular the span of the first \(n\) eigenfunctions is a Chebyshev space in the interior.''',('berardhelffer2018sturm',),fate=r'''The original theorem was announced in 1833 and published in 1836. A detailed modern proof and historical correction are given by \citet[Theorems 1.4 and 2.15]{berardhelffer2018sturm}, circulated in 2017 and revised in 2018.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',publication_match='exact',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000651','theorem','Courant’s nodal-domain bound',r'''The multidimensional comparison invokes the bound for an individual Laplace eigenfunction.''',r'''For a real eigenfunction associated to the \(n\)-th eigenvalue of a self-adjoint Laplacian on a connected compact manifold, or a bounded domain with standard Dirichlet or Neumann conditions, the number of nodal domains is at most \(n\), with eigenvalues counted with multiplicity. This theorem applies to a single eigenspace; it does not automatically extend to sums of different eigenvalues.''',('berardhelffer2018courant',),fate=r'''The classical theorem and indexing convention are restated in \citet[Theorem 1.1]{berardhelffer2018courant}. The distinction from an extended assertion is the issue raised in the next intervention.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',publication_match='exact',area='Spectral geometry')
item(391,'P000651','question','Arnold’s Harnack-type question for eigenfunction sums',r'''Arnold asks about transferring Harnack-type assertions to linear combinations of eigenfunctions. No precise numerical bound is recorded.''',r'''A natural definite comparison is the extended Courant property: a nonzero sum of eigenfunctions with eigenvalues at most \(\lambda_n\) should have no more nodal domains than the first index of \(\lambda_n\). This property is false in general. For example, the Neumann equilateral triangle admits a constant plus a second eigenfunction with three nodal domains, exceeding the first index two.''',('berardhelffer2018courant',),**{'class':'B'},reconstruction=r'''The extended Courant property is an explicit reconstruction of one plausible Harnack-type question, not a verbatim conjecture of Arnold in these notes. Other algebraic-geometric bounds may remain meaningful.''',fate=r'''\citet[Statement 1.2 and Section 3]{berardhelffer2018courant}, circulated in 2017 and published in 2018, gives this precise comparison and counterexample. It does not settle every unspecified Harnack-type inequality.''',present_status='As of November 2026, the specified extended Courant assertion is disproved. The general wording in the seminar is broader and cannot be assigned that single fate without the reconstruction caveat.',speaker='V. I. Arnold',problem='GP-1975-25',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Spectral geometry')
problem('GP-1975-24','Abel limits of Laplace spectral phases',r'''For a closed Riemannian manifold, determine when \(\varepsilon\sum_ke^{-\varepsilon\lambda_k}(\delta_{\rm per}(\mu-a\lambda_k)-(2\pi)^{-1})\) converges as a periodic distribution, and express its nonzero Fourier coefficients through closed geodesics. The source’s intended genericity assumptions are incomplete.''',['1975-391.7','1975-391.8'],r'''As of November 2026, fixed-mode closed-orbit contributions are explained by wave-trace theory. A source proving the full distributional limit under exactly the notebook’s hypotheses was not located; uniform growth and noncancellation are the explicit remaining qualifications, not an assertion that the whole wave-trace problem is open.''',['duistermaatguillemin1975spectrum','zelditch1998wave'])
problem('GP-1975-25','A Harnack-type bound for sums of eigenfunctions',r'''The definite reconstructed question is whether the extended Courant nodal-domain bound holds for linear combinations of Laplace eigenfunctions below an eigenvalue cutoff. Arnold’s notebook intervention asks more broadly for a Harnack-type extension and states no exact bound.''',['1975-391.16'],r'''The specified extended Courant property is false in general; an explicit two-dimensional Neumann counterexample is given in the 2018 paper. This is a partial match to the broader historical question. Status November 2026.''',['berardhelffer2018courant'])
