talk(392,'1976-04-05','A. M. Polyakov','P000652-P000655','Lattice Yang–Mills fields and their partition function',r'''The continuation announced on P000647 appears on P000654. Polyakov replaces continuum connections by group-valued link variables, forms plaquette products and integrates the Boltzmann weight against product Haar measure. This is an expository return to the Wilson-lattice material of 1974-345.1--8. No new explicit confinement conjecture is written on this page.''')
item(392,'P000654','definition','Continuum action and its coupling convention',r'''The action is \((4e^2)^{-1}\operatorname{tr}\int A_{\mu\nu}^2\), immediately followed by a definition of \(F_{\mu\nu}\); the commutator has no coupling constant.''',r'''After absorbing the coupling into the connection, \(F=dA+A\wedge A\) and \(S_E=-(4e^2)^{-1}\int\operatorname{tr}F_{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}\,d^4x\) for anti-Hermitian matrices. Stationarity gives \(D^\mu F_{\mu\nu}=0\). If the matrices commute, the curvature reduces to the Abelian electromagnetic expression.''',('wilson1974confinement',),reconstruction=r'''The curvature symbol in the integrand and Euclidean sign are emended; the changed coupling convention is legitimate. The Euler--Lagrange equation assumes compactly supported variations or vanishing boundary terms.''',fate=r'''This is the continuum starting point for \citet[Sections I--II]{wilson1974confinement}, already published in 1974.''',literal_correctness='false as written',area='Gauge theory')
item(392,'P000654','construction','Link variables and plaquette action',r'''Each directed link carries \(B_{x,\mu}\in SU(2)\); the four-link plaquette product enters a class function \(\chi\), called ``any character'' and then described as constant on conjugacy classes.''',r'''For link variables \(U_{x,\mu}\), set
\[U_p=U_{x,\mu}U_{x+a_\mu,\nu}U^{-1}_{x+a_\nu,\mu}U^{-1}_{x,\nu}.\]
Under \(U_{x,\mu}\mapsto\Omega_x^{-1}U_{x,\mu}\Omega_{x+a_\mu}\), the plaquette is conjugated at \(x\). Hence \(\sum_p\phi(U_p)\) is gauge invariant for every class function \(\phi\). A character is a special class function. The Wilson choice is proportional to \(\sum_p(1-\frac12\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}U_p)\).''',('wilson1974confinement',),reconstruction=r'''An arbitrary class function gives an invariant lattice action but does not automatically have the desired continuum Yang--Mills limit. The subtraction and small-plaquette quadratic normalization distinguish the Wilson choice.''',fate=r'''The link and plaquette construction is \citet[Section III]{wilson1974confinement}, received 12 June 1974 and published 15 October 1974. The source is therefore expository, not a new 1976 discretization.''',publication_match='exact',area='Gauge theory')
item(392,'P000654','definition','Finite-lattice Haar integral and correlations',r'''The notes write \(Z=\int\prod_{x,\mu}dB_{x,\mu}e^{-S(B)}\), count \(4n\) group integrations and \(12n\) variables, and leave a heading for correlation functions.''',r'''On a finite periodic four-dimensional lattice with \(n\) sites, there are \(4n\) independent positively oriented links. Normalized Haar measure on each \(SU(2)\) factor defines \(Z\); for a real continuous action, \(0<Z<\infty\). Expectations are \(\langle O\rangle=Z^{-1}\int Oe^{-S}\prod dU\). The unreduced configuration space has dimension \(12n\); the quotient by gauge transformations does not have that dimension.''',('wilson1974confinement',),fate=r'''\citet[Section III]{wilson1974confinement} provides the gauge-invariant lattice integral. Existence at finite lattice spacing and volume follows from compactness and does not establish an infinite-volume or continuum limit.''',publication_match='exact',area='Gauge theory')
item(392,'P000654','method','Wick rotation and the continuum functional integral',r'''The schematic replacement \(t\to it\), \(e^{iS}\to e^{-S}\), is followed by ``in the continuous case---a functional integral''. No measure is constructed.''',r'''Imaginary-time continuation motivates the Euclidean Boltzmann factor after the action and integration contour are transformed consistently. A rigorous reconstruction of a Lorentzian theory requires additional analytic and positivity properties. The finite product Haar integral is an actual measure; the displayed continuum functional integral remains formal here.''',('wilson1974confinement','jaffewitten2000yangmills'),fate=r'''The Euclidean lattice strategy predates the talk \citep[Section III]{wilson1974confinement}; the continuum existence problem is stated separately by \citet[Section 4]{jaffewitten2000yangmills}.''',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',publication_match='partial',area='Gauge theory')
