talk(393,'1976-04-12','D. B. Fuchs','P000653-P000657','Computer calculations in Hamiltonian Lie-algebra cohomology',r'''Fuchs describes the polynomial cochain model, its finite weight decomposition, the exotic Gelfand--Kalinin--Fuchs classes and Perchik's constant-term Euler series. The two definite questions are to compute the whole cohomology and to prove nonvanishing of the relative Euler characteristic in unbounded weights. Buchstaber's short intervention on P000656 concerns coalgebra structures arising from multiplication and averaging. Its placement is preserved within this catalogue record. The constant-term calculation is developed with a finite, explicitly checked scope in Section~\ref{dev:perchik}.''')
item(393,'P000655','definition','The Hamiltonian cochain complex in polynomial symbols',r'''The opening calls the algebra formal divergence-free vector fields, then writes alternating polynomials in pairs \((\xi_i,\eta_i)\), with differential built from \(\langle\xi_i,\eta_j\rangle-\langle\xi_j,\eta_i\rangle\).''',r'''The displayed complex is the continuous Chevalley--Eilenberg complex of
\[\mathfrak{ham}_{2n}=\mathbb R[[p_1,\ldots,p_n,q_1,\ldots,q_n]]/\mathbb R\]
with the Poisson bracket. A cochain is represented by a polynomial alternating under exchange of argument pairs and vanishing when any entire pair is zero. Writing \(z_i=(\xi_i,\eta_i)\) and \(\omega(z_i,z_j)=\langle\xi_i,\eta_j\rangle-\langle\xi_j,\eta_i\rangle\), the source convention is
\[(dP)(z_1,\ldots,z_{q+1})=\sum_{i<j}(-1)^{i+j-1}\omega(z_i,z_j)P(z_i+z_j,z_1,\ldots,\widehat z_i,\ldots,\widehat z_j,\ldots,z_{q+1}).\]
The order of the remaining arguments is unchanged.''',('kotschickmorita2009gkf',),reconstruction=r'''The quotient by constants supplies the omitted reduced-cochain condition. In two variables divergence-free formal fields are Hamiltonian; in higher even dimension the two Lie algebras differ. The Poisson factor, not the opening shorthand, identifies this complex.''',fate=r'''The algebra and continuous weight convention are explicitly fixed in \citet[Section 2]{kotschickmorita2009gkf}. The displayed differential is the dual of the Poisson bracket, so its square is zero by Jacobi.''',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655','question','Computing the full Hamiltonian cohomology',r'''The underlined task is to find the cohomology of the displayed complex, said to remain undone even for \(n=1\).''',r'''Determine the continuous cohomology \(H^*(\mathfrak{ham}_{2n};\mathbb R)\), including its graded dimensions and multiplicative structure. The first unresolved case intended here is \(\mathfrak{ham}_2\), not the Lie algebra of all formal vector fields in one variable.''',('mikami2016metoki','moritasakasaisuzuki2015computations'),**{'class':'C'},fate=r'''Later computations and factorizations give substantial finite-weight information. \citet[p.151 and Theorem 1.2]{mikami2016metoki} still states the infinite-dimensionality question and proves the Metoki-class factorization. Stable-rank graph-homology calculations concern a different limiting object \citep[Remark 2.1]{moritasakasaisuzuki2015computations}.''',present_status='As of 10 November 2026, targeted searches located no complete fixed-rank computation or resolution of the infinitude question. The latest explicitly verified open-status statement used here is from 2016; this search result is not a proof of present-day nonexistence.',problem='GP-1975-21',worth='The finite weight complexes permit exact calculations, but a full answer needs a mechanism valid in unbounded weights.',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655','construction','The all-vector-field comparison',r'''The notes say that taking polynomials linear in \(\eta\) gives the cochain complex of \(W_n\).''',r'''Fibre-linear Hamiltonians \(H_v(p,q)=\sum_i p_i v_i(q)\) form a Lie subalgebra isomorphic, up to the fixed Poisson-sign convention, to the Lie algebra \(W_n\) of formal vector fields on \(\mathbb R^n\). Restricting Hamiltonian cochains to this subalgebra retains the symbol component linear in each fibre variable. This is a restriction map of complexes; those polynomial components need not form a subcomplex of the unrestricted Hamiltonian-symbol complex without projection.''',('gelfandfuchs1970formal',),reconstruction=r'''The restriction interpretation makes the informal sentence precise and prevents the next sentence, ``not done even for \(n=1\)'', from being misapplied to \(W_1\).''',fate=r'''The cohomology of \(W_n\) had already been calculated by \citet[main cohomology theorem]{gelfandfuchs1970formal}, received 4 November 1969 and published in 1970. The full original English article was opened; the present distinction follows from the displayed fibre-linear embedding.''',publication_match='partial',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655','method','Finite bigraded complexes and off-diagonal contraction',r'''The total degrees in \(\xi\) and \(\eta\) are \(m_1+q\) and \(m_2+q\). Each \(K_{m_1,m_2}\) is finite-dimensional, and \(m_1\ne m_2\) is acyclic.''',r'''The differential preserves the two shifted degrees. For fixed \(n,m_1,m_2\), only finitely many exterior monomials occur: the weight-minus-one translation space is finite-dimensional, the quadratic weight-zero space is finite-dimensional, and every other factor has positive weight. The Hamiltonian \(\sum_i p_iq_i\) acts on this complex by the scalar \(m_1-m_2\), up to sign. Cartan's identity gives a contracting homotopy on a nonzero eigenspace, hence
\[H^*(\mathfrak{ham}_{2n})=\bigoplus_m H^*(K_{m,m}).\]''',('kotschickmorita2009gkf',),reconstruction=r'''\ours\ The finiteness argument and contraction are supplied explicitly. The formal-cochain topology is continuous cohomology; an unrestricted algebraic dual would be a different object.''',fate=r'''The finite weight construction agrees with \citet[Section 2]{kotschickmorita2009gkf}. The source already states the reduction; the argument here explains why it is valid.''',publication_match='partial',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655','theorem','Low weights and the exotic Gelfand–Kalinin–Fuchs classes',r'''For \(n=1\), the notes give acyclicity at \(m=1,2,3\) and nonzero cohomology in degrees 7 and 10 at \(m=4\), followed by a machine-memory failure at \(m=5\).''',r'''In total Hamiltonian weight \(w=2m\), the exotic relative Gelfand--Kalinin--Fuchs class lies in \(H^7(\mathfrak{ham}_2,\mathfrak{sp}_2)_8\). The corresponding absolute complex has classes in degrees 7 and 10. The statement about exhausted computer memory is a report about a calculation, not a cohomological vanishing or nonvanishing assertion.''',('gelfandkalininfuchs1972hamiltonian','kotschickmorita2009gkf','mikami2016metoki'),fate=r'''The exotic class was published in Russian in 1972. Its later factorization is recalled in \citet[Theorem 1.1]{mikami2016metoki}: a degree-five, weight-ten class for fields vanishing at the origin is multiplied by the transverse symplectic class. Theorem 1.2 similarly factors the Metoki class of degree nine and weight fourteen, first reported in 1999.''',publication_match='exact',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655','construction','Relative cohomology and the omitted zero-weight exception',r'''A subcomplex \(K'_{m,m}\) is introduced with \(H(K_{m,m})=H(K'_{m,m})\otimes H(S^3)\); its Euler characteristic is initially denoted \(\chi_m^*\), then loses the star.''',r'''For the plane, \(K'_{m,m}\) is the complex relative to the quadratic Hamiltonians \(\mathfrak{sp}_2\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2\), in weight \(2m\). For \(m\ne0\), the absolute cohomology is the relative cohomology plus its degree-three shift. At weight zero, absolute cohomology is in degrees 0 and 7, whereas relative cohomology is in degrees 0 and 4. Thus the blanket tensor-product statement needs an exception, and the nontrivial Euler series is relative.''',('mohammadzadehfuchs2014cohomology',),fate=r'''The exception and the absolute/relative comparison are explicit in \citet[Section 1 and Theorem 2.1]{mohammadzadehfuchs2014cohomology}. The lost star changes the mathematical question, since the absolute Euler characteristic is zero.''',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='exact',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655-P000656','construction','Perchik’s constant-term Euler series',r'''Perchik is credited with a generating function whose constant term in \(x\) is \(t^{-2}+2-t^8-t^{14}-t^{22}+\cdots+10t^{70}+\cdots\). The general row of factors contains an ambiguous middle exponent of \(t\).''',r'''Put \(E_m=\chi H^*(\mathfrak{ham}_2,\mathfrak{sp}_2)_{2m}\). Then
\[\sum_{m\ge-1}E_mt^{2m}=\frac12\operatorname{CT}_x\left[(1-t^{-1}x^{-1})(1-t^{-1}x)(1-x^{-2})(1-x^2)\prod_{\ell\ge1}\prod_{j=0}^{\ell+2}(1-t^\ell x^{-\ell-2+2j})\right].\]
The infinite product has a well-defined formal Laurent expansion in \(t\) with lower bound \(-2\). The linear Hamiltonians supply the two negative-weight factors; deleting them gives a different Lie algebra, \(\mathfrak{ham}_2^0\).''',('perchik1976cohomology','kotschickmorita2009gkf'),reconstruction=r'''The symbol \(\operatorname{CT}_x\) formalizes ``discard everything containing \(x\)''. Every factor in the weight-\(\ell\) row has \(t^\ell\). Section~\ref{dev:perchik} gives an independent invariant-character derivation and finite cutoff proof.''',fate=r'''Perchik's work was his 1975 Harvard dissertation and appeared in Topology in 1976. The relative weight interpretation is corroborated by \citet[Section 5]{kotschickmorita2009gkf}; its Remark 9 has an inconsistent superscript in describing the full series, so that shorthand is not followed.''',publication_match='exact',development='developed in this chapter',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000655-P000656','question','Infinitely many nonzero relative Euler characteristics',r'''Fuchs asks to prove that \(\chi_m\ne0\) for arbitrarily large \(m\). The question follows the relative-complex notation and Perchik's product.''',r'''For the coefficients \(E_m\) of the preceding fixed-rank relative Euler series, prove or disprove that \(\{m\ge1:E_m\ne0\}\) is infinite. A positive answer implies infinitely many nonzero weight components of Hamiltonian cohomology; the converse does not follow, since even and odd cohomology can cancel in an Euler characteristic.''',('perchik1976cohomology','mikami2016metoki'),**{'class':'C'},fate=r'''Later finite calculations do not settle this unbounded-weight assertion. The related infinite-dimensionality question is explicitly retained by \citet[p.151]{mikami2016metoki}. The present chapter develops only a rigorous finite truncation and exact coefficient verification.''',present_status='As of 10 November 2026, no resolution of this exact coefficient-infinitude question was located in the recorded targeted searches. The chapter’s computation through m=150 supplies finite evidence and no claim about all later weights.',problem='GP-1975-20',worth='A closed asymptotic or structural argument for the constant-term series would turn finite evidence into an infinitude theorem.',development='developed in this chapter',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000656','claim','The numerical lower bound of 57 cohomology classes',r'''The expansion through \(t^{70}\) is said to prove the existence of at least 57 nontrivial cohomology classes.''',r'''For each finite relative weight complex, \(\sum_q\dim H^q_{2m}\ge|E_m|\). The computed coefficients satisfy
\[\sum_{m=-1}^{35}|E_m|=57.\]
Thus the total relative cohomology has dimension at least 57 when these weights, including weight zero and the negative weight, are included. The positive weights alone give 54. The number does not mean 57 positive-weight primitive exotic generators.''',('perchik1976cohomology',),reconstruction=r'''\oursComputed\ The chapter independently reproduces this finite sum and \(E_{35}=10\), then extends the verified range through \(m=150\). The inequality is the elementary comparison of the alternating and ungraded dimensions of a finite complex.''',fate=r'''This supplies a precise interpretation and independent check of the notebook's 57. The development does not equate an Euler coefficient with a cohomological dimension or a count of algebra generators.''',publication_match='partial',development='developed in this chapter',area='Lie-algebra cohomology')
item(393,'P000656','construction','Multiplication dualized to a coproduct',r'''Buchstaber starts with \(X\times X\to X\) for a genuine group and writes a map \(C(X)\to C(X\times X)\) as a coalgebra structure.''',r'''For a compact Hausdorff topological group, multiplication gives \(\Delta f(x,y)=f(xy)\) on continuous functions, with counit evaluation at the identity. Associativity gives coassociativity. In this setting \(C(X\times X)\) is a completed tensor product; an algebraic tensor-product coalgebra is obtained instead on a suitable algebra of representative functions.''',('buchstaber1975twovaluedI',),reconstruction=r'''The topology and tensor-product convention are supplied. Arbitrary continuous functions need not lie in the algebraic tensor product \(C(X)\otimes C(X)\).''',fate=r'''The algebra/coalgebra viewpoint leads directly into \citet[Section 3]{buchstaber1975twovaluedI}, received 2 December 1974 and published in 1975. The notebook gives only the opening construction, not a complete theorem about all topological spaces.''',speaker='V. M. Buchstaber',publication_match='partial',area='Formal groups')
item(393,'P000656','construction','Averaging a two-valued formal multiplication',r'''The next line replaces a value of \(f(x\cdot y)\) by its mathematical expectation. The heading says that Buchstaber is reporting Adams on Buchstaber.''',r'''For a commutative two-valued formal group law over a \(\mathbb Q\)-algebra, with branches \(F_+(x,y),F_-(x,y)\), the natural linear coproduct is
\[\Delta f=\tfrac12\bigl(f(F_+(x,y))+f(F_-(x,y))\bigr).\]
The symmetric expression is interpreted through the formal law even when its branches are not separately in the base power-series ring. Associativity of the two-valued law gives coassociativity of this averaging map, which generally is not an algebra homomorphism.''',('buchstaber1975twovaluedI',),reconstruction=r'''This is a concrete, explicitly labelled identification of the expectation notation, supported by the contemporaneous two-valued-formal-group literature. The fragment does not itself specify the number of values or the full axioms.''',fate=r'''The construction is read in \citet[Section 3 and the generalized translations of Section 4]{buchstaber1975twovaluedI}. The bibliographical lead Adams--Liulevicius, ``Buhstaber's work on two-valued formal groups'' (1975), matches the heading, but its full text was not accessible and no detailed theorem is attributed to it here.''',speaker='V. M. Buchstaber',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Formal groups')
problem('GP-1975-20','Infinitely many nonzero coefficients in Perchik’s relative Euler series',r'''For the fixed-rank plane Hamiltonian Lie algebra, determine whether the relative Euler coefficients \(E_m=\chi H^*(\mathfrak{ham}_2,\mathfrak{sp}_2)_{2m}\) are nonzero for infinitely many positive integers \(m\). The series includes the linear Hamiltonians and is not the series for \(\mathfrak{ham}_2^0\).''',['1975-393.8'],r'''No resolution was located as of 10 November 2026. The bounded development derives the product rigorously and verifies all coefficients through m=150 by two independent exact methods; this does not prove infinitude.''',['perchik1976cohomology','mikami2016metoki'])
