talk(395,'1976-04-19','I. M. Gelfand, after L. N. Vaserstein; V. G. Drinfeld','P000656-P000658','Serre’s problem and Suslin’s resultant method',r'''Gelfand, following Vaserstein's notes, asks the projective-module and unimodular-row forms of Serre's problem. Drinfeld gives the monic-column reduction and Suslin's resultant translation lemma. These notes belong to the period when the independent solutions were already circulating: Suslin's paper was received in February and presented in March, and Quillen's was received on 1 March 1976. The earlier three-variable problem GP-1973-33 recurs here in arbitrary dimension.''')
item(395,'P000657','question','Serre’s problem over a polynomial ring',r'''The question is whether every projective \(\mathbb C[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\)-module is free. The word projective is added in a callout.''',r'''Every finitely generated projective module over \(k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), for a field \(k\), is free. The finite-generation hypothesis is the standard form of Serre's problem. The source asks over \(\mathbb C\), while the solution applies to every field.''',('suslin1976projective','quillen1976projective'),fate=r'''The affirmative solutions are \citet[Section III, Theorems 2--3]{suslin1976projective} and \citet{quillen1976projective}. Suslin's manuscript was received 26 February and presented 12 March 1976; Quillen's was received 1 March. Both precede this meeting, although publication followed in 1976. Suslin's theorem was read; Quillen's publisher chronology was checked.''',present_status='As of November 2026, the stated finite-generation problem is solved. The earlier three-variable occurrence is subsumed by this theorem.',problem='GP-1973-33',publication_match='exact',area='Algebraic K-theory')
item(395,'P000657','definition','Unimodular rows and determinant-one completion',r'''A row \((a_1,\ldots,a_k)\) is unimodular if \(\sum_i p_i a_i=1\). The question asks for a matrix of determinant one with this first row.''',r'''Let \(A=K[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), with \(K\) a field, and let the row length be at least two. A unimodular row gives a split surjection \(A^k\to A\), whose kernel is a finitely generated projective module. Its freeness gives a matrix completing the row; rescaling one of the other rows makes the determinant one. For row length one, a unit is the only entry, and determinant one requires that unit to equal one.''',('suslin1976projective',),reconstruction=r'''The field symbol in this statement should be read independently of the source's row length; the mechanism is the split kernel. Completing all unimodular rows proves freeness of stably free modules, so equivalence with the entire projective-module problem also uses the known stable-freeness input.''',fate=r'''The completion follows from \citet[Section III, Theorems 2--3]{suslin1976projective}; the determinant adjustment is elementary. The exceptional row length one is omitted in the notebook.''',problem='GP-1973-33',publication_match='partial',area='Algebraic K-theory')
item(395,'P000657','theorem','A monic unimodular column is equivalent to its constant value',r'''Drinfeld takes a commutative coefficient ring \(B\), a unimodular column \(f\in B[x]^n\), and assumes that \(f_1\) has leading coefficient one. He states \(f(x)\sim f(0)\).''',r'''For a commutative ring with identity, a unimodular polynomial column with a monic component lies in the same \(\mathrm{GL}_n(B[x])\)-orbit as its value at zero. For \(n\ge3\), a resultant patching argument proves this; unimodular pairs are directly completable, and the length-one case is immediate. Evaluation at zero preserves unimodularity.''',('monceuryengui2017suslin',),reconstruction=r'''The relation means multiplication by an invertible polynomial matrix. The coefficient ring need not be a field. A monic component is the crucial additional hypothesis, not an assertion that arbitrary projective modules over arbitrary rings are extended.''',fate=r'''The general-ring resultant input is \citet[Theorem 3.1 and Lemma 4.1]{monceuryengui2017suslin}, tracing Suslin's 1977 Lemmas 2.1 and 2.3. The notebook is an earlier witness to this method; the modern algorithm's simplifying infinite-field hypothesis is not imposed on the general existence lemma.''',speaker='V. G. Drinfeld',publication_match='exact',area='Algebraic K-theory')
item(395,'P000657','definition','The resultant and its leading coefficients',r'''The resultant is introduced as the product of differences of the roots of two univariate polynomials.''',r'''For \(f=a_m\prod_{i=1}^m(x-\xi_i)\) and \(g=b_n\prod_{j=1}^n(x-\eta_j)\) over a splitting field,
\[\operatorname{Res}(f,g)=a_m^n b_n^m\prod_{i,j}(\xi_i-\eta_j).\]
The Sylvester determinant defines it over any commutative coefficient ring and gives a polynomial identity \(\operatorname{Res}(f,g)=uf+vg\) for suitable polynomials \(u,v\). The unweighted root product is correct when both polynomials are monic.''',('monceuryengui2017suslin',),reconstruction=r'''The leading coefficients are restored, and the determinant definition is needed because arbitrary rings need not admit root factorizations.''',fate=r'''The resultant and its Bézout identity are the algebraic mechanism in \citet[Lemma 4.1]{monceuryengui2017suslin}. The notebook's abbreviated root formula is insufficient for the arbitrary-ring argument.''',speaker='V. G. Drinfeld',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',area='Algebraic K-theory')
item(395,'P000658','theorem','Suslin’s translation by a resultant',r'''With \(c=\operatorname{Res}(f_1,f_2)\), the main lemma says \(f(Y_1+cY_2)\sim f(Y_1)\) for any polynomial column. The proof is credited to an explicit construction by Suslin.''',r'''Let \(B\) be a commutative ring with identity and \(f\in B[x]^n\), \(n\ge2\). With \(c=\operatorname{Res}(f_1,f_2)\), there is an invertible matrix over \(B[Y_1,Y_2]\) carrying \(f(Y_1)\) to \(f(Y_1+cY_2)\). No unimodularity assumption on this column is required for the lemma itself. The two-component construction can be chosen in \(\mathrm{SL}_2\); the remaining components are adjusted using the Bézout expression for \(c\).''',('monceuryengui2017suslin',),reconstruction=r'''The quantifiers and coefficient ring are made explicit. The lemma is not the assertion that an arbitrary resultant is a unit.''',fate=r'''The two-component matrix and identity are read in \citet[Lemma 4.1, p.117]{monceuryengui2017suslin}, which identifies this as Suslin's 1977 Lemma 2.1. The seminar attributes the construction to Suslin already in April 1976.''',speaker='V. G. Drinfeld',publication_match='exact',area='Algebraic K-theory')
item(395,'P000658','method','Patching translations and eliminating variables',r'''The notes say one needs \(c=1\), then puts \(Y_2=-Y_1\); a change of variables makes a component monic, and variables are removed one at a time.''',r'''The missing step is to obtain several resultants \(c_i\) generating the unit ideal, after allowed operations on a monic unimodular column. Choose \(d_i\) with \(\sum_i d_i c_i=1\); successive translations by \(d_i c_iY\) combine to a translation by \(Y\). Specializing \(Y=-x\) gives equivalence to the value at zero. Over a field, a triangular change of polynomial variables can make a nonzero component monic in the last variable, after rescaling its leading coefficient, and this permits induction on the number of variables.''',('monceuryengui2017suslin','suslin1976projective'),reconstruction=r'''\ours\ The composition of translations explains the unit-ideal patching. It does not assume the original pair has resultant one. The monic-changing assertion is stated over a field, where the required nonzero leading coefficient is invertible.''',fate=r'''The needed resultant ideal is \citet[Theorem 3.1]{monceuryengui2017suslin}; Section 4 supplies a constructive version. The conclusion belongs to the 1976 solution of Serre's problem, not to an unresolved question left by the missing lines.''',speaker='V. G. Drinfeld',publication_match='partial',area='Algebraic K-theory')
