talk(396,'1976-04-19','S. P. Novikov','P000657-P000658','Relativistic fluids, coordinate freedom and Gödel’s metric',r'''The final record resumes the Einstein--fluid equations, gives the sound-speed inequalities, explains the apparent equation deficit by coordinate freedom, and mentions Gödel's closed timelike curves. The four-entry signature fixes the intended spacetime dimension despite the notation M-n. The chapter boundary follows immediately after this discussion.''')
item(396,'P000658','definition','Perfect-fluid data and normalization of velocity',r'''The page writes \(M^n\) with signature \((1,-1,-1,-1)\), repeats the perfect-fluid stress and explicitly imposes \(\langle u,u\rangle=1\).''',r'''The intended setting is \(M^4\) with a Lorentz metric, a unit future-directed timelike velocity, energy density and pressure satisfying an equation of state. In units \(c=1\), the unknown fields are constrained by \(G=T\), \(T_{ij}=(\varepsilon+p)u_i u_j-pg_{ij}\), and stress conservation. The source's unit normalization supplies a hypothesis left implicit in record394.''',('fouresbruhat1958fluids',),fate=r'''This is the pre-existing relativistic perfect-fluid system, with the convention found in \citet[Section 9]{fouresbruhat1958fluids}. The repeated spherical-symmetry aside refers back to 1975-394.11--12.''',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='exact',area='General relativity')
item(396,'P000658','claim','Sound speed and relativistic causality',r'''The sound speed is \(c_s^2=\partial p/\partial\varepsilon\ge0\), with \(c_s^2\le c^2=1\). Dust has \(p=0\).''',r'''For a barotropic perfect fluid in units \(c=1\), linear acoustic disturbances in the rest frame have \(c_s^2=dp/d\varepsilon\). Nonnegative squared speed is the elementary local stability requirement; \(c_s\le1\) keeps the acoustic cone inside the light cone. Strict hyperbolicity assumptions often use \(0<c_s^2<1\) and \(\varepsilon+p>0\). The zero-speed dust case requires its own formulation, so the weak inequalities alone are not a complete well-posedness theorem.''',('fouresbruhat1958fluids',),reconstruction=r'''\ours\ Linearizing the energy and momentum equations about constant fluid at rest gives \(\partial_t^2\delta\varepsilon-(dp/d\varepsilon)\Delta\delta\varepsilon=0\), which fixes the sound-speed formula.''',fate=r'''The fluid characteristic cone is identified in \citet[Section 11, p.168]{fouresbruhat1958fluids}. The source states physical inequalities, not all analytical assumptions for the coupled Cauchy problem.''',publication_match='exact',area='General relativity')
item(396,'P000658','method','Coordinate freedom, constraints and hyperbolic reduction',r'''The notes say there are too few equations, but gauge transformations compensate; a historical aside says Hilbert rejected the equations as incorrect.''',r'''Four coordinate freedoms and four contracted Bianchi identities prevent treating the ten Einstein equations as ten independent scalar evolution equations in an arbitrary chart. Harmonic coordinates give a quasilinear hyperbolic reduced system. If the initial Einstein constraints and harmonic gauge conditions hold, stress conservation propagates the gauge constraints, and the reduced solution solves the original equations. Geometrical uniqueness is understood up to diffeomorphism.''',('fouresbruhat1952cauchy','fouresbruhat1958fluids'),reconstruction='This is the precise local PDE interpretation of the equation count. It does not substantiate the anecdote about Hilbert, whose wording is retained only as source provenance.',fate=r'''The smooth local Cauchy problem was established by \citet[Introduction and gravitational application]{fouresbruhat1952cauchy}. The constraint-propagation calculation is explicit in \citet[Section 2]{fouresbruhat1958fluids}. Both substantially predate the meeting.''',publication_match='partial',area='General relativity')
item(396,'P000658','construction','Gödel’s spacetime and closed timelike curves',r'''Novikov mentions a Gödel metric with a closed timelike line, without displaying a metric or matter model.''',r'''Gödel's homogeneous rotating cosmological spacetime solves Einstein's equation with pressureless dust and a nonzero cosmological constant. It contains closed timelike curves. In the usual cylindrical coordinates of the original construction, circles at sufficiently large radius are timelike. The cosmological constant must be included, or absorbed into an effective stress tensor, to compare this example with the preceding equation lacking an explicit cosmological term.''',('godel1949cosmological',),fate=r'''The metric, dust source and cosmological term are given in \citet[Sections 2--3]{godel1949cosmological}, published 1 July1949; Section3 exhibits the timelike circles. The original four-page article was read.''',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',publication_match='exact',area='General relativity')
correction('P000658','dimension and signature','M^n with four-entry signature','M^4 in this discussion','The scan confirms n, but both the signature and velocity have exactly four components.')
save()

