item(391,'P000651','claim','Ground-state positivity and the polynomial analogy',r'''The first eigenfunction is described as positive everywhere; successive eigenfunctions are compared with a linear function and a conic before the nodal-set questions.''',r'''For the scalar Laplacian on a connected closed manifold the lowest eigenvalue is zero and its eigenfunctions are constant, so a ground state can be chosen strictly positive. For a connected bounded domain with Dirichlet conditions, the first eigenfunction is strictly positive in the interior after choosing its sign, and vanishes on the boundary. The comparisons of later eigenfunctions with linear and quadratic polynomials are an analogy, not a general classification of their nodal sets.''',('berardhelffer2018courant',),reconstruction=r'''Connectedness, the boundary condition and the choice of sign are supplied; ``everywhere'' must be restricted to the interior in the Dirichlet case.''',fate=r'''This is the standard ground-state comparison underlying the nodal discussion; \citet[Introduction and Theorem1.1]{berardhelffer2018courant}. The later-eigenfunction analogy does not add a theorem beyond the precise Sturm and Courant assertions.''',speaker='A. I. Shnirelman',publication_match='partial',area='Spectral geometry')
